// retro-casio-sk1 — an SFZ kit of k7-rack: the SK-1 sampling keyboard's drums, two Univox sounds and four strays in its top notes, notes 24–35.
// One octave of Retro Drums, The Tic Tok Men's sampled drum machines ("free use" per its README),
// cleaned up by S. Christian Collins. One sample per sound: no velocity layers, no round-robins.
// The kit file holds six machines, one per octave; each is its own engine, drawing only its pads.
// The samples (~2 MB) are not in the library: the engine's machine fetches them at start-up.
extends("@engine/sfz-player")
params({
  sfz: "@instruments/retro-drums/Tic Tok Men RetroDrums 1.sfz",
  // Its loudest hit at full velocity peaks at 0 dBFS — the level a rack's normalised samples play at.
  Volume: 2.4,
})
// The pads its panel draws, on the notes the kit file plays them on.
kit({
  Kick: 24,
  HatClosed: 25,
  Snare: 26,
  HatOpen: 27,
  UnivoxSnare: 28,
  TomLow: 29,
  UnivoxHatOpen: 30,
  TomHigh: 31,
  ShortHat: 32,
  Kick2: 33,
  Crash: 34,
  ShortSnare: 35,
})
// The Casio SK-1 sampling keyboard's lo-fi drums, with two Univox sounds and four extras — Retro Drums by The Tic Tok Men. Its samples, about 2 MB, download from the k7 tray's Downloads tab.
